  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nRidging continue to dominate the central portions of the country. \nThe center of the ridge has shifted off to the south and west \nslightly compared to the past couple of days as a shortwave passes \nacross the Great Lakes region into the Northeast. Overnight showers \nand thunderstorms that followed the mid/upper level thermal ridge \ncontinue to linger across southeast Kansas early this afternoon. \nWidespread mid level clouds have kept temperatures at bay so far \nthis afternoon. But as clouds continue to clear out, we're going to \nsee a late day rebound in temperatures with afternoon highs reaching \naround 100 degrees for southern Kansas with locations in central \ninto eastern Kansas only reaching into the lower to middle 90s.\n\nFor tonight through Friday, as the forecast area sits on the \nnortheastern edge of the mid/upper ridge, small perturbations in the \nflow will lead to rain and storm chances each night. Diurnal \nconvection that develops on the High Plains during the afternoon \nhours will travel across central into southeastern Kansas each night \ninto the morning hours. Due to the overall warm column, severe \nweather is not anticipated with tonight or Wednesday night's \nactivity. However, when a more potent shortwave passes across \nthe Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest on Thursday night, a \nweak frontal boundary will sweep across the state. This setup \nwill support more widespread showers and storms into Friday \nmorning. While severe weather is not widely anticipated, the \nsynoptic forcing combined with the moderately unstable \nenvironment may support some stronger wind gusts and heavy rain.\n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week will remain slightly \nabove normal for this time of year as we remain solidly under the \ninfluence of the ridge. Afternoon highs will reach into the middle \nto upper 90s for most with locations in far south-central Kansas \ntopping out around 100 each day. Peak heat index values will also \nreach into the 100-105 degree range for many across the forecast \narea. We will continue to not receive much relief during the \novernight hours as lows will only fall into the middle to upper 70s \neach night. \n\nThen for Saturday through the end of the forecast period, slightly \nbelow normal temperatures are expected this weekend behind the \n\"cold\" front. The ridge will be further shunted to the west and the \nforecast area will be under northerly flow. Afternoon highs across \nthe region look to reach into the upper 80s to lower 90s with \novernight lows falling into the 60s. Heights then look to gradually \nincrease for early next week with temperatures creeping back into \nthe lower to middle 90s in response. A much drier airmass will also \nfilter in along with the more mild temperatures with PWATs around 1\" \nfor this weekend into early next week. This will limit rain chances \nand lead to mild RH values in the 30% range, meaning it will feel \ndownright pleasant with heat indices around 90 degrees.",
